  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved skills development strategies for employability</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO supports Tanzania in addressing skills mismatches and enhancing workforce employability to achieve its long-term socio-economic development goals. This initiative strengthens national capacities to align education and training systems with current and future labor market demands, ensuring that skills development strategies are inclusive, flexible, and responsive.

In mainland Tanzania, the program focuses on bridging gaps in the Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) system, addressing low levels of highly skilled labor, and promoting skills relevant to industrialization. Efforts include fostering partnerships between the public and private sectors, improving skills governance, and expanding work-based learning opportunities, including quality apprenticeships and Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL).

In Zanzibar, the initiative supports human capital development by improving access to education and training for disadvantaged groups, including women, youth, rural populations, and persons with disabilities. The program emphasizes entrepreneurship, lifelong learning, and industry-ready graduates through apprenticeships, on-the-job training, and innovative skills strategies.

The ILO facilitates the development of modular apprenticeship programs, national skills strategies, and labor market information systems (LMIS). It also strengthens institutional capacities and promotes social dialogue mechanisms to ensure inclusive and sustainable outcomes. This comprehensive approach aims to empower Tanzanian youth and workers to access decent work opportunities and contribute to economic growth and resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
